What you may do
---------------

  * Redistribute them as part of PunchThemAll, unchanged — in a modpack, on a
    server, in a mirror of the mod. No permission needed, no credit required.
  * Use them to refer to the mod: screenshots, pack pages, videos, wikis.
  * Fork the mod and keep them, as long as the fork is still recognisably
    PunchThemAll rather than a different mod wearing its face.

What you may not do
-------------------

  * Ship them in an unrelated project.
  * Sell them, or sell access to them.
  * Use them as the identity of another mod, so that players cannot tell which
    project they are looking at.

The distinction that matters is the last one. The code is MIT because reuse of
code is good for everyone; the artwork is narrower because it is what tells
players whose mod this is.

No third-party assets
---------------------

PunchThemAll ships no assets it did not make. In particular it contains no
Minecraft textures, sounds or models: everything it draws in JEI and EMI is
either its own artwork or a block/item stack rendered by the game at runtime
from the player's own installation.

Mojang's assets are not redistributable, so if you fork this mod, do not add
any — the temptation is a copied vanilla texture used as a placeholder, and it
is a licence problem rather than a shortcut. (One had crept into this repository
and was removed in 2.4.0.)
